Certainly! Below is an example of how you might implement a simple raycasting algorithm in GLSL. This example assumes that we have a 2D grid representing the world, where each cell can either be empty or contain an obstacle.

### Vertex Shader

The vertex shader will pass through the position of each vertex without modification since this is a basic example:

```glsl
#version 330 core

layout(location = 0) in vec3 aPos;

void main()
{
    gl_Position = vec4(aPos, 1.0);
}
```

### Fragment Shader

The fragment shader will perform the raycasting logic to determine if there's an obstacle at the current pixel location:

```glsl
#version 330 core

out vec4 FragColor;

uniform sampler2D uWorldTexture; // Texture containing the world data
uniform float uCellSize;         // Size of one cell in the world texture
uniform int uWidth;              // Width of the world texture
uniform int uHeight;             // Height of the world texture

// Function to check if a point is inside an obstacle
bool isObstacle(vec2 pos)
{
    return texture(uWorldTexture, pos / uCellSize).r > 0.5;
}

void main()
{
    vec2 uv = gl_FragCoord.xy / uResolution.xy; // Normalized coordinates [0, 1]

    // Cast rays from the center of the screen towards the edges
    for (int i = -1; i <= 1; ++i)
    {
        for (int j = -1; j <= 1; ++j)
        {
            if (i == 0 && j == 0) continue; // Skip the central ray

            vec2 dir = normalize(vec2(i, j));
            vec2 start = uv + dir * 0.01; // Start slightly offset from the center

            while (true)
            {
                vec2 nextPos = start + dir * uCellSize;

                if (nextPos.x < 0 || nextPos.x >= 1.0 ||
                    nextPos.y < 0 || nextPos.y >= 1.0)
                {
                    break; // Out of bounds
                }

                if (isObstacle(nextPos))
                {
                    // Found an obstacle, draw something here
                    FragColor = vec4(1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0); // Red color for obstacles
                    return;
                }

                start += dir * uCellSize;
            }
        }
    }

    // No obstacles found, clear the fragment
    FragColor = vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0); // Black background
}
```

In this example:
- `uWorldTexture` is a texture that contains the world data, with each pixel representing whether it's an obstacle (`> 0.5`) or not (`<= 0.5`).
- `uCellSize`, `uWidth`, and `uHeight` define the size and dimensions of the cells in the world texture.
- The raycasting loop casts rays from the center of the screen towards the edges, checking for obstacles along the way.

This is a very simplified version of raycasting and may need adjustments based on your specific requirements and environment setup.

