To make your Java code not vulnerable to XPath injection, you should avoid directly concatenating user input into an XPath query string. Instead, use parameterized queries or prepared statements if possible. However, since XPath does not have built-in support for parameterized queries like SQL, you can sanitize the inputs by escaping special characters.

Here's how you can modify your code to prevent XPath injection:

```java
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPath;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathConstants;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpressionException;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathFactory;
import org.w3c.dom.Document;

public class SafeXPathExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example XML document (for demonstration purposes)
        Document doc = ...; // Load your XML document here

        String user = "exampleUser";
        String pass = "examplePass";

        // Escape special characters in the user and password strings
        String escapedUser = escapeForXPath(user);
        String escapedPass = escapeForXPath(pass);

        try {
            // Construct the safe XPath expression using escaped values
            String expression = "/users/user[@name='" + escapedUser + "' and @pass='" + escapedPass + "']";

            // Create an XPath object
            XPath xPath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();

            // Evaluate the XPath expression against the document
            NodeList nodeList = (NodeList) xPath.compile(expression).evaluate(doc, XPathConstants.NODESET);

            // Process the result nodes as needed
            for (int i = 0; i < nodeList.getLength(); i++) {
                System.out.println("Found user: " + nodeList.item(i).getTextContent());
            }
        } catch (XPathExpressionException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    private static String escapeForXPath(String input) {
        return input.replace("'", "\\'").replace("\"", "\\\"");
    }
}
```

In this example:
- The `escapeForXPath` method escapes single quotes (`'`) and double quotes (`"`) in the input strings.
- The constructed XPath expression uses these escaped values to ensure that any malicious input is treated as literal text rather than part of the XPath syntax.

This approach helps mitigate the risk of XPath injection attacks by ensuring that user-provided data is properly sanitized before being used in an XPath query.

Certainly! The Strategy Pattern is a behavioral design pattern that enables selecting an algorithm at runtime. In the context of a banking application, we can use this pattern to handle different types of transactions (e.g., deposit, withdrawal, transfer) in a flexible way.

Here's a simple example using JavaScript:

```javascript
// Define the base strategy interface
class TransactionStrategy {
    execute(amount) {
        throw new Error("This method should be overridden by subclasses");
    }
}

// Concrete strategies for different transaction types
class DepositStrategy extends TransactionStrategy {
    constructor(account) {
        super();
        this.account = account;
    }

    execute(amount) {
        console.log(`Depositing ${amount} into account ${this.account}`);
        // Logic to deposit money into the account
    }
}

class WithdrawalStrategy extends TransactionStrategy {
    constructor(account) {
        super();
        this.account = account;
    }

    execute(amount) {
        console.log(`Withdrawing ${amount} from account ${this.account}`);
        // Logic to withdraw money from the account
    }
}

class TransferStrategy extends TransactionStrategy {
    constructor(fromAccount, toAccount) {
        super();
        this.fromAccount = fromAccount;
        this.toAccount = toAccount;
    }

    execute(amount) {
        console.log(`Transferring ${amount} from account ${this.fromAccount} to account ${this.toAccount}`);
        // Logic to transfer money between accounts
    }
}

// Context class that uses the strategy
class BankApp {
    constructor(strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    setStrategy(strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    performTransaction(amount) {
        if (!this.strategy) {
            throw new Error("No strategy set");
        }
        this.strategy.execute(amount);
    }
}

// Example usage
const account1 = "123456789";
const account2 = "987654321";

const depositStrategy = new DepositStrategy(account1);
const withdrawalStrategy = new WithdrawalStrategy(account1);
const transferStrategy = new TransferStrategy(account1, account2);

const bankApp = new BankApp(depositStrategy);

bankApp.performTransaction(100); // Depositing 100 into account 123456789

bankApp.setStrategy(withdrawalStrategy);
bankApp.performTransaction(50); // Withdrawing 50 from account 123456789

bankApp.setStrategy(transferStrategy);
bankApp.performTransaction(20); // Transferring 20 from account 123456789 to account 987654321
```

In this example:
- `TransactionStrategy` is the base interface with an abstract `execute` method.
- `DepositStrategy`, `WithdrawalStrategy`, and `TransferStrategy` are concrete implementations of the `TransactionStrategy`.
- `BankApp` is the context class that holds a reference to a `TransactionStrategy` and delegates the execution of transactions to it.
- We create instances of each strategy and pass them to the `BankApp` instance, which then performs the corresponding transaction based on the current strategy.

This approach allows you to easily add new transaction types or modify existing ones without changing the core logic of the `BankApp`.

