Next, configure Vue Query in your main file (e.g., `main.js` or `main.ts`) by creating a new instance of `QueryClient` and wrapping it around your app using `QueryClientProvider`.

```javascript
// main.js
import { createApp } from 'vue';
import App from './App.vue';
import { createPinia } from 'pinia';
import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/vue-query';

const queryClient = new QueryClient();

createApp(App)
  .use(createPinia())
  .use(queryClient)
  .mount('#app');
```

Now, inside your component where you want to fetch data from `/get_session`, import necessary modules and hooks provided by `@tanstack/vue-query`. Use the `useQuery` hook to make an API call and map the desired fields into your Pinia store upon successful retrieval.

Here is an example implementation:

```javascript
<template>
  <!-- Your template code here -->
</template>

<script setup>
import { ref, onMounted } from 'vue';
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/vue-query';
import { useSessionStore } from '@/stores/session'; // Adjust path accordingly

const sessionStore = useSessionStore();

function fetchSessionData() {
  return axios.get('/get_session'); // Replace with actual endpoint URL
}

