Here's an example of a recursive JavaScript function that retries a request up to three times before resolving the promise:

```javascript
function retryRequest(requestFunction, maxRetries = 3) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const attempt = (retryCount) => {
            requestFunction()
                .then(resolve)
                .catch((error) => {
                    if (retryCount < maxRetries) {
                        console.log(`Attempt ${retryCount + 1} failed. Retrying...`);
                        setTimeout(() => attempt(retryCount + 1), 1000); // Retry after 1 second delay
                    } else {
                        console.error('Max retries reached. Request failed.');
                        reject(error);
                    }
                });
        };

        attempt(0); // Start with the first attempt
    });
}

// Example usage:
const makeRequest = () => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        // Simulate a network request or any asynchronous operation
        const success = Math.random() > 0.5; // Randomly decide whether the request succeeds or fails

        if (success) {
            resolve('Request succeeded!');
        } else {
            reject(new Error('Request failed!'));
        }
    });
};

retryRequest(makeRequest)
    .then((result) => {
        console.log(result);
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        console.error(error.message);
    });
```

In this code snippet, `retryRequest` is a higher-order function that takes another function (`requestFunction`) as its argument and returns a new promise. The inner `attempt` function handles each individual attempt at making the request.

If the request fails, it will log a message indicating which attempt has failed and then call itself recursively after waiting for one second using `setTimeout`. This process continues until either the request succeeds or the maximum number of retries (`maxRetries`) is reached.

The example usage demonstrates how you can use this function by providing your own `makeRequest` function that simulates a network request or any other asynchronous operation. If the request succeeds within the allowed number of attempts, the result will be logged; otherwise, an error message will be displayed.

